1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,987
Assembly, test, and the rack.

2
00:00:02,937 --> 00:00:06,600
This is episode six of From Sand to GPU.

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:11,212
A CoWoS-class package is still not what most hyperscalers receive.

4
00:00:11,212 --> 00:00:17,000
The path runs from wafer sort through package assembly and final test, then into trays and racks.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:24,362
For modern NVIDIA platforms, the shippable unit is often a tray or an NVL72 rack — not a bare BGA.

6
00:00:24,362 --> 00:00:27,662
Wafer sort comes first at front-end exit.

7
00:00:27,662 --> 00:00:31,137
Electrical test identifies known-good die.

8
00:00:31,137 --> 00:00:38,250
For large GPU dies, sort strategy and any repair heavily affect effective yield — that sensitivity is likely.

9
00:00:38,250 --> 00:00:42,475
Bad die that slip through become expensive scrap after packaging.

10
00:00:42,475 --> 00:00:52,025
Package assembly continues the bonding story: substrate attach, ball attach, lids or heat spreaders where the product needs them, then marking, baking, and dry-pack.

11
00:00:52,025 --> 00:01:00,187
Final test uses package-level automated test equipment for stuck-at faults, speed bins, HBM interface training, and power rails.

12
00:01:00,187 --> 00:01:05,687
Burn-in and high-temperature operating-life sampling support reliability qualification.

13
00:01:05,687 --> 00:01:15,137
System-level test is increasingly important for AI GPUs — a likely industry trend, because package ATE alone does not catch every rack bring-up failure.

14
00:01:15,137 --> 00:01:19,212
Board and rack assembly is where ODMs and OEMs take over.

15
00:01:19,212 --> 00:01:26,950
They build GPU baseboards and compute trays, NVLink switch trays, power shelves, coolant-distribution units, and full racks.

16
00:01:26,950 --> 00:01:35,462
Cabling and blind-mate midplanes matter; Vera Rubin materials emphasize modular, cable-free trays — confirmed in NVIDIA’s Technical Blog.

17
00:01:35,462 --> 00:01:47,975
Partners listed for Vera Rubin availability include major clouds and OEMs such as Dell, HPE, Lenovo, Supermicro, Quanta, Wistron, and Foxconn — confirmed from NVIDIA Newsroom in March twenty-twenty-six.

18
00:01:47,975 --> 00:02:06,525
Materials at this stage include packaged GPU, CPU, switch, NIC, and DPU components; high-layer server PCBs; connectors, cold plates, manifolds, and quick-disconnects; VRMs and bulk capacitors; thermal interface materials and EMI shields; plus firmware, BMC, and security roots of trust.

19
00:02:06,525 --> 00:02:14,712
Supplier map: TSMC advanced packaging finishes CoWoS; OSATs such as ASE, Amkor, and JCET may handle adjacent products.

20
00:02:14,712 --> 00:02:18,400
ATE leaders include Teradyne and Advantest.

21
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:22,062
ODM and OEM names dominate rack throughput.

22
00:02:22,062 --> 00:02:28,562
Liquid-cooling ecosystem players such as Vertiv and CoolIT appear in industry maps — treat that set as likely.

23
00:02:28,562 --> 00:02:30,887
Yield risk shifts late.

24
00:02:30,887 --> 00:02:35,137
Discovering HBM lane failures after packaging is costly.

25
00:02:35,137 --> 00:02:42,075
Rack-scale products push yield into system bring-up — NVLink training, power sequencing, and thermal integrity.

26
00:02:42,075 --> 00:02:47,450
Counterfeit or gray-market board components remain a general electronics tracker risk.

27
00:02:47,450 --> 00:02:56,100
NVIDIA’s Vera Rubin materials describe hot-swappable trays, NVLink resiliency, and Mission Control operational software — confirmed.

28
00:02:56,100 --> 00:03:02,687
Partner product availability is described as starting in the second half of twenty-twenty-six — also confirmed.

29
00:03:02,687 --> 00:03:10,825
Exact OSAT split outside TSMC for Rubin final package test versus NVIDIA or ODM system-level test remains unknown.

30
00:03:10,825 --> 00:03:21,875
Trackers should watch ATE lead times, ODM rack throughput, liquid-cooling quick-disconnect and manifold shortages, system-level-test farm utilization, and partner general-availability timing.

31
00:03:21,875 --> 00:03:26,162
That is episode six: from known-good die to a shippable rack.

32
00:03:26,162 --> 00:03:32,262
Next time: equipment, materials, and gases — the CapEx calendar behind every bottleneck.

